<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1" ProductVersion="3.1.4026.19">
  <DUT Name="ST_FG_03_Transfer_Status" Id="{502cb990-41d8-44d2-b458-07242aa9ccac}">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[// What the two grip sensors have to say beyond the PackML state.
//
// FG_03 HAD NO FAULT CODE AT ALL until B_Detect1 and B_Detect2 arrived, because it
// had nothing to report: every step waited on a cylinder limit, and a cylinder
// limit cannot fail in a way the cylinder itself does not already alarm. A part
// sensor can - it is the first thing in this group that can disagree with the
// actuators.
//
//   1  stuck at   - Resetting could not separate the two grippers in the handover
//      handover     window: arm 2 reported its jaws open and B_Detect2 still saw
//                   the part. Nothing was moved, because every way out of that
//                   window pulls the two arms apart with one part between them.
//                   The arms are still standing at the handover; free the part by
//                   hand, then Clear and Reset.
//   2  no pick    - Y_Gripper2 closed on slot 1 and B_Detect2 never confirmed a
//                   part. An empty slot, a mispositioned pallet, or a grip that
//                   closed on nothing - which reaches its extended limit SOONER
//                   than a real grip, so the limit alone cannot tell them apart.
//   3  no handover- Y_Gripper1 closed at the handover and B_Detect1 never confirmed.
//                   This is the dangerous one: without it, step 90 opens arm 2
//                   against a grip that was never made and the part falls.
//   4  no release - a gripper opened and its sensor still sees the part, inside
//                   Execute. The part is jammed in the jaws.
//   5  off safe   - a cycle was started with a part held by an arm that is NOT at
//                   its safe position (X and Y retracted). The resume entry point
//                   is derived from which gripper is full, and it is only right if
//                   the arm is where the reset would have parked it. Someone jogged
//                   it in Manual, or code 1 is standing. Reset fixes it, because
//                   Resetting reaches a safe position from anywhere.
//
// WHY CODE 1 DOES NOT ABORT OUT OF Resetting. A reset must terminate on time and on
// its own cylinders - transport-behaviour.md 4, learned twice the expensive way.
// Aborting mid-reset would leave the arms wherever they happened to be, which is
// worse than stopping cleanly and saying so. Resetting completes; Execute refuses
// the next cycle through code 5, because a part stuck at the handover is by
// definition not at a safe position.
//
// ResumePoint is not a fault. It is which sub-sequence the NEXT Start will enter,
// derived every scan from the two grip sensors - the same derivation Execute step 0
// makes, published so the panel can show it before anyone presses anything:
//
//   1  the whole cycle, from the pick out of slot 1  (both grippers empty)
//   2  the handover, from arm 2's safe position      (B_Detect2)
//   3  the placement into slot 2, from arm 1's       (B_Detect1)
//
// Cleared in FG_03.Clearing - the fault code, that is. ResumePoint is derived and
// has nothing to clear.
TYPE ST_FG_03_Transfer_Status :
STRUCT
	FaultCode	: DINT;
	ResumePoint	: DINT;
END_STRUCT
END_TYPE
]]></Declaration>
  </DUT>
</TcPlcObject>
